Why is `super()` used?


Is there a difference between using `Base.__init__` and `super().__init__`?


```
class Base(object):
    def __init__(self):
        print "Base created"

class ChildA(Base):
    def __init__(self):
        Base.__init__(self)

class ChildB(Base):
    def __init__(self):
        super(ChildB, self).__init__()

ChildA() 
ChildB()
```

